WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
 - Hello, and welcome to chapter two.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:04.560
 Now we're gonna continue to talk about

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.600
 the building blocks of Python, variables, constants,

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.080
 statements, expressions, et cetera.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.440
 The first thing we have to talk about is constants.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.280
 These are just things we call them constants

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.720
 'cause they don't change.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.200
 They're numbers, strings, et cetera,

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.120
 and we use them to sort of start calculations

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.820
 or if something is greater than 40 hours,

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:26.720
 we're gonna do something,

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.040
 and so 40 is the constant in that situation.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.440
 So we have 123, we have 98.6,

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.680
 we have hello, world, which is a string

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.240
 by enclosing it in quotes.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.840
 We pass each of these things to the print function,

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.560
 and a side of respect to the print function

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.140
 is that we see the output.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:46.520
 So print 123, prints out 123, print 98.6, prints it out.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.760
 So these are just really the syntax of constants,

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.320
 and without constants, we can't write really much of anything.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.520
 The other sort of foundational notion

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.080
 of any programming language are the reserved words,

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.840
 and like I said before, reserved words

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.680
 are these special words where Python is listening for them,

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.700
 and there is very special meaning.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:10.200
 So when Python sees if, it's not just any other word,

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.860
 it means how Python implements conditional execution.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:18.440
 Variables are the third building block,

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.800
 and that is a way that you can ask Python

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.280
 to allocate a piece of memory and then give it a name,

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:26.840
 and you can put stuff in that.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.200
 Sometimes you just put one value,

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.960
 later we'll see when we do collections in chapters eight

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.800
 and nine, we will see the more than one value

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.160
 can be put into a variable, and the variable,

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:38.760
 how we control the variable is through

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.280
 the assignment statement, and as I said before,

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.720
 it's important to think of the assignment statement

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.200
 as having an arrow to it.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.960
 So this is not saying X for all time is the same as 12.2,

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.960
 what it's saying is take 12.2, find a place,

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.080
 find some memory in your computer there, Mr. Python,

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.480
 give it a label X, we get to choose the X,

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.000
 that's the variable part, we chose it, right?

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.040
 And then stick 12 in it, and then the same is true for 14.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.920
 Go find another spot, name it Y,

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.760
 and then put a 14 in there.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.960
 So think of this as an arrow every time you see

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:15.800
 that equality in an assignment statement.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.960
 Now, these variables hold one value.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.160
 So now if we have these three statements, these two,

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.000
 and then the third one executes, it says put 100 into X,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.200
 but that wipes out the old value of 12.2,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.120
 and it rewrites it with 100.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.960
 And so we can change the variables,

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.960
 that's another reason that we call them variable.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:43.120
 There are some names, some rules for making variable names,

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.480
 you can start with a letter or an underscore.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.440
 We tend not to, as normal programmers use underscore,

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.640
 we tend to reserve those for variables

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.040
 that we use to communicate with Python itself.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.280
 So when we're making up a variable,

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.800
 we tend not to use underscores as the first character.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.520
 You can have letters and numbers and underscores

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.040
 after the first character, and they're case sensitive,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.840
 but it's really a bad idea to use case

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:10.400
 as the only differentiator.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.360
 So in this case, spam eggs, spam 23 and underscore speed

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.160
 are all totally legit, we would probably not use this one

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.760
 unless we were actually doing it

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.440
 because Python told us to use that variable.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.440
 23 spam starts with a number, pound sign starts,

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.180
 and dot is not a legitimate variable character.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:32.840
 And spam, capital spam, and all cap spam are different,

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.920
 but this is not something that you want

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.120
 to sort of depend on too much.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.480
 So that's just the rule names,

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:42.240
 we tend to start them with a letter,

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.960
 and then use letters, numbers, and underscores.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.720
 Underscores, other than the first character,

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.840
 are generally pretty common,

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.400
 and you'll see those used a lot.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.240
 Now, when we're choosing variable names,

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.240
 one of the things about variables

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:54.400
 is we get to choose the name.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.400
 We get to choose the name X, choose the name Y.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.480
 And so sometimes we like them short,

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.120
 but sometimes we want them descriptive,

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.000
 and the notion that of making variables descriptive

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.000
 is often confusing to beginning students.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.480
 Sometimes it's really helpful

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.320
 to if you're gonna have a line of text

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.080
 and you name the variable line, that's great,

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.080
 because the next person reading your program says,

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.160
 "Oh, that must be the line of text."

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.760
 Whereas it also can become misleading

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.400
 that the name of a variable somehow has meaning.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.680
 So sometimes we'll have singular variables

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:29.360
 and plural variables, like friend and friends.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.440
 You're like, "Is plural, does Python know

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.320
 "about singular and plural?"

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:34.600
 And the answer is no.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.720
 So sometimes we pick variables that make no sense,

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.240
 sometimes we pick variables that make a lot of sense.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.760
 This is just something that you as a beginning programmer

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:46.200
 are going to have to understand that we can pick anything

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:50.280
 we want, and so you'll see, I'll try to call attention

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.720
 to this in the first few lectures as we go through.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.040
 So here's a bit of code with an assignment statement,

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.000
 two assignment statements,

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.720
 a multiplication and a print statement.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:01.600
 And you can say, "What is this doing?"

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.360
 Now, Python is perfectly happy with this code,

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:06.440
 'cause it assigns it in there.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:09.160
 You have said, "Please go give me this as a label,"

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:10.680
 and then we assign two variables,

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.320
 and then we're carefully pulling these two variables

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.200
 back out, multiplying them together,

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.240
 and sticking them into yet another variable,

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:18.520
 and then printing that variable out.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:21.160
 That seems like we can figure out what it is,

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:22.800
 you just have to look really carefully,

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:24.640
 and a single character mistake,

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:29.520
 and Python is gonna be pretty unhappy, okay?

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.840
 So that's one way to write this program.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:35.200
 It's hard, though, because any of those characters

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.040
 are long variables, and they're random stuff.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:41.880
 It's not very friendly to anyone who might read your program.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.200
 Now, this looks a little friendlier.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:44.360
 It's the same program,

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.960
 because Python just wants a correspondence.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:49.560
 You picked A, you picked B, and you picked C,

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:53.240
 and it's really much easier for us to see what's going on.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:58.240
 And so this is, in a way, going from here to here

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.960
 is much friendlier, but we can be even friendlier

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:03.560
 if we pick mnemonic variable names.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:07.160
 So this is not mnemonic, this is short and convenient.

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:09.000
 This is long and inconvenient.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.120
 Python is happy with any of these.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:12.840
 Here, on the other hand,

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:15.440
 is another version of the exact same program.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.640
 And now you think to yourself, oh, yeah, now I get it.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:22.440
 35 is the number of hours, $12.50 is the rate,

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.800
 and then we're gonna multiply the hours in the rate

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.200
 and come up with a pay, and we're putting out the pay.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.200
 Now, whoever wrote this program is helping us greatly

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.120
 understand what's going on, and that's good.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:35.680
 Choosing variable names.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:39.120
 But Python, again, all three of these are the same to Python.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.760
 Choosing variable names in a way that help your reader

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:45.080
 understand what's going on is a great thing.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:50.040
 The problem is, the danger is, if you read this

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.960
 and you think that somehow Python understands payroll,

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.520
 that if you name a variable hours,

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:56.680
 that Python knows what hours means.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:59.720
 The answer is, Python really doesn't care

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:00.680
 what you name the variable,

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.920
 as long as what you name it, you use it, right?

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.160
 And so you gotta be careful,

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:09.400
 and so you'll see, I will, when I write my code

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:13.120
 in these first few weeks, first few lectures,

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.480
 I will sometimes write it with gibberish,

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.160
 I'll sometimes write it with extremely short

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:18.480
 but meaningless variable names,

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:21.120
 and sometimes I'll use meaningful variable names,

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:24.640
 and I'll call your attention to it, and it will get you.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:26.920
 You'll start, when you look at this third kind,

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:30.640
 it has meaningful variables or mnemonic variable names,

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.640
 you'll just instinctively want to give Python

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:36.640
 more intelligence than it sort of deserves,

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:39.200
 I guess that's probably the best way to say that.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:41.120
 So, we've talked about constants,

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.520
 we've talked about reserved words,

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:43.920
 we've talked about variables.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:48.040
 And so, here we have a sentence,

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:49.760
 like we've already done some of these things,

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:53.800
 where we set x equals two, we retrieve the old value of x

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:56.560
 and add two to it, so that becomes four,

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:57.680
 and then we print four out.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:00.040
 Print is a function that's built in,

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:02.040
 and we pass in whatever we want to print out.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:05.960
 So, this parentheses is part of a function call.

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:09.760
 Okay, so, an assignment statement,

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:13.200
 you have to really get your head around the notion

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:16.440
 that it has this arrow nature,

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:19.760
 and that it evaluates this entire right-hand side

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:23.120
 before we change the left-hand side.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:24.920
 And so, you can think of this sort of as,

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:26.760
 at time step one, it does this,

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:28.960
 and then at time step two, it does the copy.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.600
 And that's how you can have something like x

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:35.520
 on both sides of an assignment statement.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:37.480
 And so, if, for example, we have x,

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:42.480
 and x has 0.6 in it, x has 0.6 in it,

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.720
 what happens is, is that it first,

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.880
 it sort of ignores this part right here,

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:48.400
 and evaluates the expression.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.440
 So, it pulls the 0.6, everywhere x appears,

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:55.160
 it pulls 0.6 out, then it starts running these calculations,

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:57.200
 and then it has the new value.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:59.160
 After all the calculations are done,

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:04.160
 then, and only then, is it going to put that back into x.

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:07.800
 And so, it sort of takes that and puts it back into x,

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:10.320
 and then wipes out the old value.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.400
 At this point, this has all been taken care of,

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:15.680
 and it's been reduced down to this 0.93,

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:19.320
 and so, that is what's put in as the new value.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:23.000
 So, up next, we'll talk a little bit more

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.880
 about making more complex expressions.

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:30.480
 (gentle piano music)

